# Patterns of System Change

Meadows describes several fundamental patterns through which systems change over time: evolutionary change (gradual, incremental), revolutionary change (sudden, transformative), growth, stability, decline, chaos, and collapse. These patterns recur across ecological, economic, social, and personal systems.

## Key Insight

Understanding which pattern a system is following—and which pattern it is transitioning toward—enables more effective anticipation and intervention. Revolutionary change often follows prolonged periods of evolutionary buildup.

## Connections

Patterns of system change connect to [[system-dynamics]], [[leverage-points]], [[complex-adaptive-systems]], and [[nonlinear-behavior]]. They complement [[scenario-planning]] and [[decision-making-under-uncertainty]] by providing structural archetypes for forecasting.
